  "place": "Berkeley, United States",
  "nameOrigin": "After Dubna, the Russian town that is home to the Joint Institute for Nuclear Research.",
  "symbolNote": "Berkeley proposed hahnium (Ha) and Dubna nielsbohrium (Ns); IUPAC proposed joliotium (Jl) in 1994 before adopting dubnium (Db) in 1997. Temporary name: unnilpentium (Unp).",
  "source": "iupac_discovery",
  "doi": "10.1351/pac199365081757",
  "note": "The 1993 IUPAC/IUPAP report found Berkeley's and Dubna's 1970 reports practically simultaneous and of equal weight, so credit is shared; PubChem gives 1967, the year of Dubna's first report.",
  "placeLocation": {
   "lat": 37.874,
   "lon": -122.271,
   "source": "natural_earth"
  }
 },
 "prose": {
  "text": "Dubnium is a synthetic element in group 5, below tantalum, named after the Russian town of Dubna, whose laboratory reported it in 1967; a Berkeley team claimed it independently in 1970. The rivalry fed years of argument over names, and the Berkeley proposal, hahnium, was eventually abandoned. Only a few atoms are made in any experiment, yet dubnium-268 is remarkably durable for so heavy a nucleus: its half-life of about 28 hours is the longest of any known isotope beyond lawrencium. Theory predicts the outer configuration [Rn] 5f¹⁴ 6d³ 7s² and a ⁴F₃/₂ ground level, the same pattern as tantalum, but neither has been measured.",
  "sources": [
   "pubchem",
   "iaea_livechart",
   "nist_gsie"
  ]
 }
